/* book exam traps: Ribs and spars swapped. Ribs run chordwise and keep the aerofoil shape, passing load to the spars; spars run spanwise and take the bending. | Treating the skin as shape only. In semi-monocoque construction the skin is a primary load path and carries pressure, shear and part of the bending. | Frames and longerons swapped. Frames are rings that hold the cross-section; longerons are longitudinal members that take bending. | Calling every ring a bulkhead. A bulkhead is a solid partition, and a pressure bulkhead carries the whole cabin pressure. | Using the full spar depth or the skin-to-skin depth in F = M/h. Use the distance between the cap centroids; a larger h gives a cap force that is too small. | Pressure unit slip. 15 kPa is 15 000 Pa, not 15 Pa; a wrong conversion makes the answer 1000 times too small. */
