/* book: lead: A fuselage or wing must carry loads and keep its shape. Three classic arrangements do this: a truss of bars covered with fabric, a monocoque where the skin alone carries everything, and a semi-monocoque where the skin works with a skeleton. The semi-monocoque wins for modern aircraft because it lets the skin stay thin while stringers and longerons take bending. Only a few universities examine this */
